# Knowledge Preservation

## Objective

Record important decisions, gotchas, and non-obvious context somewhere durable, so future work (by AI or humans) doesn't have to rediscover it from scratch.

## Core Principles

- Record non-obvious 'why' decisions in code comments, ADRs, or docs — not only in chat history that will be lost.
- When discovering a project gotcha (e.g. 'this endpoint is rate-limited at 10rps'), write it down where the next person will see it.
- Keep a single source of truth for a given piece of knowledge rather than scattering it across chats.
- Prefer durable artifacts (README, ADR, code comment) over relying on the AI's or a person's memory of a past conversation.

## Requirements

- When a design decision required back-and-forth to arrive at, summarize the conclusion into a comment or doc, not just the chat.
- When encountering an undocumented but important project constraint, add a note for future work.
- Reference existing ADRs/docs when available instead of re-deriving decisions from scratch.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Making an important architectural decision that lives only in an ephemeral chat conversation.
- Rediscovering the same project gotcha repeatedly because it was never written down.
- Duplicating the same explanation across multiple docs that can drift out of sync.

## Self Review

Before considering work complete, verify:

✓ Non-obvious decisions/gotchas discovered during the task are captured somewhere durable.
✓ No important context was left only in conversation history.
✓ Existing documented knowledge was checked/reused before re-deriving it.

## Golden Rule

If it took real effort to figure out, it's worth 30 seconds to write down.
